<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE DEPENDENCY VULNERABILITY GATE. These three properties are the whole of it, and before
    a separate change none of them was written down: the audit ran on SDK defaults, so it gated by
    inheritance rather than by declaration. It worked - but an SDK upgrade could have moved the
    severity floor or narrowed the transitive scope with no diff in this repo and no failing
    build to announce it. Declared here at the values that were already in effect (measured with
    `dotnet msbuild src/AgentForge.Api/AgentForge.Api.csproj -getProperty:NuGetAuditMode`), so
    changing the gate now takes an edit somebody has to review.

    NuGetAudit      restore consults nuget.org's advisory data and warns on a match.
    NuGetAuditMode  `all` - transitive packages too. Every advisory this repo has actually hit
                    came in transitively (Microsoft.OpenApi via AspNetCore.OpenApi,
                    System.Security.Cryptography.Xml via EntityFrameworkCore.Design), so `direct`
                    would have caught none of them. See the pins in Directory.Packages.props.
    NuGetAuditLevel `low` - the floor is the lowest severity NuGet publishes, i.e. report
                    everything. This is the only value that
                    needs no argument about which advisories are worth knowing about: raising it
                    is a decision to be blind to a class, and it would be reviewable here.

    THIS IS ONLY HALF THE GATE. An advisory is a WARNING; `TreatWarningsAsErrors` above is what
    turns NU1903 into a red build. Either half alone is a no-op. So never silence an advisory with a
    NoWarn, a WarningsNotAsErrors, NoWarn metadata on a PackageReference or a <NuGetAuditSuppress>
    item, and never redeclare these properties - here, in a second PropertyGroup below, or in a nested
    Directory.Build.props, which MSBuild takes INSTEAD of this file for everything beneath it. Fix an
    advisory by bumping or pinning the package in Directory.Packages.props. Edit the one declaration
    above; `dotnet msbuild <project> -getProperty:NuGetAuditMode` reads back what a project evaluates to.
    reference: CONVENTIONS.md §2
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
